  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 1255 PM CST Sun Nov 1 2026\n\nA strong mid level system will impact the region Monday into\nTuesday, featuring a deep 500mb trough with potent shortwave\nenergy interjecting into the trough. This results in some\nuncertainty in finer details of synoptic evolution, but there is\nhigh confidence in seeing strong forcing and impressive wind\nfields with the system.\n\nWinds Monday into Tuesday: Strong southerly surface and low\nlevel flow will result in sustained winds of 15-25 mph, with \ngusts up to 35 mph. Strongest winds Monday will be west of Hwy\n65, shifting east overnight to the eastern CWA on Tuesday\nmorning. Winds decrease below notable levels on Tuesday\nafternoon.\n\nSevere:\n\nOverall expecting a slight risk for severe storms along/west of\nHwy 65 late Monday into Monday night as shown by the updated \nSPC outlook. As storms move east late Monday night into Tuesday,\nconditions will not be as favorable for severe weather, with\nmarginal risk noted. Main risks for be damaging winds and brief,\nisolated tornadoes.\n\nInstability will be a significant limiting factor, with even\nMUCAPE values generally 250 J/kg or less (locally up to 500 \nJ/kg possible, but less likely). This should limit any isolated\nto scattered afternoon WAA-forced convection to remain below\nsevere limits. However, upstream convection from central OK to \ncentral KS is expected to grow upscale into QLCS structures as \npotent shortwave energy and a strengthening LLJ move into the \nregion. Strong forcing from height falls and PVA aloft, along \nwith low level convergence, are expected to overcome the weak \ninstability and effectively force the linear convection. This \nleads to primarily a strong wind threat (up to 60-65 mph) and \nisolated, weak tornado threat in northeastward bowing segments \ngiven SW 0-3km shear around 30-40 kts. This threat should \nmaterialize in the evening to early overnight Monday, though \nguidance still varies on exact timing. Tuesday timing should be \nmainly in the morning to early afternoon over the eastern CWA, \nbut the severe threat may linger into the afternoon depending on\nnear-term details.\n\nIt's worth noting that, while highly unlikely, if greater\nsurface heating occurs on Monday, the instability would be\ngreater and the threat picture could change considerably. Stay\ntuned and check for updates to the DSS Packet at\nweather.gov/sgf.\n\nPrecip amounts:\n\nLinear convective elements and nearly line-parallel flow will\nlead to significant variance in amounts. Generally expect\n0.5-1.5 inches, with locally higher amounts of 1.5-2.5 inches\nper REFS PMM, and lower amounts as low as 0.25 inches. No\nsignificant flooding is expected.\n\nLate week:\n\nThe rest of the forecast is currently dry, but models do show\nvarying levels of shortwave energy moving through that could\nresult in some minor rainfall. Overall, look for cooler\ntemperatures with highs in the 60s and lows in the 30s to mid\n40s. Expect frost/freeze potential over at least the eastern\nOzarks, but that shouldn't be a shock since it's early November.",
